[{:db/id "q-39647/selection/d15397dead9ec66a753ce3d163bd79346a1104706700ec4dd723d8c76a578a0d" :answer/id #uuid "d7f5b5b9-10d9-526c-a963-7592f4529187" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $29$th term" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39647/selection/17d71c0fff766c08747a6e8dc5db505d32766694bbcd3cb4acbdbaf9946a64ed" :answer/id #uuid "5f2ba960-5c63-5d47-8ddf-5a5d1378b3e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $33$rd term" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39647/selection/33783dc5e43d1dc3ad506d3b274e4a9f1cc1f948c333325a85a82113e124dae1" :answer/id #uuid "49f5a01d-ffef-53a8-82e5-f1801fab0a14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $31$st term" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39647/selection/3031dac3285a30acab764d1b9a179ffade17150589edb1e6d49e0e5c1f45320c" :answer/id #uuid "83764edb-7364-51cf-92c6-979103a11600" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $28$th term" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39647/selection/f373f5f9f1a597c7214ba66858b35c7d42c1409089fcd5037a9ba1ce83eb8057" :answer/id #uuid "71a53ab3-8f97-5ada-95a5-03e5e3640e3d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $30$th term" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39647/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b15c552f-65d4-55ac-8ad4-877c643eb48a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-39647/selection/f373f5f9f1a597c7214ba66858b35c7d42c1409089fcd5037a9ba1ce83eb8057" :answer-field/choices ["q-39647/selection/d15397dead9ec66a753ce3d163bd79346a1104706700ec4dd723d8c76a578a0d" "q-39647/selection/17d71c0fff766c08747a6e8dc5db505d32766694bbcd3cb4acbdbaf9946a64ed" "q-39647/selection/33783dc5e43d1dc3ad506d3b274e4a9f1cc1f948c333325a85a82113e124dae1" "q-39647/selection/3031dac3285a30acab764d1b9a179ffade17150589edb1e6d49e0e5c1f45320c" "q-39647/selection/f373f5f9f1a597c7214ba66858b35c7d42c1409089fcd5037a9ba1ce83eb8057"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39647" :question/id #uuid "fd214d9a-0991-5f60-9033-e3b27bd16696" :question/math-academy-id "q-39647" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,2$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n18,\\,12,\\,8,\\,\\frac{16}{3},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=18$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{12}{18}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,2.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,2 \\\\ 18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <0.000\\,2 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.000\\,2}{18} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1≈29.159\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $30$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-39647/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39647"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-39648/selection/4b60e960f4ff07a2be9818e150751f26b21108c27877fa3ce1a40ad9a52408e8" :answer/id #uuid "5325ed31-0f99-5215-a287-dd1376b00ad1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $39$th term" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39648/selection/d936efd403be2ac358eda95559347b08db5a8a4475e3b284530fef823fcd4381" :answer/id #uuid "2d2f8df5-3daf-5db3-a4bb-c853206ac374" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $40$th term" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39648/selection/ae749b3039d17bf57e4dc3daeb286261b56f6b4df6cb77e953abe144fbbde799" :answer/id #uuid "049fcf9e-c6cd-552e-a56a-4ccd5e1f0fc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $41$th term" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39648/selection/f38f9c9ab8032ef6c9bd5e3821d1c81f7aa37880cce4d7324e73ca7f79959a63" :answer/id #uuid "1e1dc434-81fc-5bf1-9afb-edf94074adac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $38$th term" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39648/selection/1d3bb5605190c4b3009724f541b2187dec5ad4d7083dfc24b9e6d7f1859a2ca1" :answer/id #uuid "b0b1cdb4-29f1-5dc0-ab23-c6457479c2b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the $42$th term" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39648/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e05c44cf-98fe-594d-b145-a389f2f62043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-39648/selection/d936efd403be2ac358eda95559347b08db5a8a4475e3b284530fef823fcd4381" :answer-field/choices ["q-39648/selection/4b60e960f4ff07a2be9818e150751f26b21108c27877fa3ce1a40ad9a52408e8" "q-39648/selection/d936efd403be2ac358eda95559347b08db5a8a4475e3b284530fef823fcd4381" "q-39648/selection/ae749b3039d17bf57e4dc3daeb286261b56f6b4df6cb77e953abe144fbbde799" "q-39648/selection/f38f9c9ab8032ef6c9bd5e3821d1c81f7aa37880cce4d7324e73ca7f79959a63" "q-39648/selection/1d3bb5605190c4b3009724f541b2187dec5ad4d7083dfc24b9e6d7f1859a2ca1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39648" :question/id #uuid "954f45fc-f0a3-5593-bd6c-65e4d48ff69e" :question/math-academy-id "q-39648" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.001$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,48,\\,36,\\,27,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{48}{64}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.001 \\\\ 64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <0.001 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.001}{64} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1≈39.386\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $40$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-39648/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39648"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-39651"] :question/problem "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $75$, and the common ratio is $5.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,713?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=75$ and  $r=5$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{3-1} \\\\ 75 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ 75 & =25{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,713.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{n-1} & >1\\,713 \\\\ {5}^{n-1} & >571 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{5}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(571) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(571) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1≈4.944\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,713.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-278083/field-1/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" :answer/id #uuid "985354db-aab4-569f-abdb-a04db389ad3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278083/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "5f13faa1-be04-516c-b8c8-1b475c261afa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278083/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6bf432-fcef-5d5c-a367-5047cd0ec924" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-278083/field-1/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" :answer-field/choices ["q-278083/field-1/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" "q-278083/field-1/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-278083" :question/id #uuid "5b117fef-9e1e-5bc1-8b1c-bfb4109c98b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-278083" :question/problem "The $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $81$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $10\\,000$ is the{{field-1}} th term." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{5}=81$ and  $r=\\frac{3}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{5-1} \\\\ 81 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4} \\\\ 81 & =\\frac{81}{16}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $10\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >10\\,000 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >625 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(625) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(625) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈16.877.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $17$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $10\\,000.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-278083/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-278083"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-278089/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "618879e2-79a5-5f42-add0-dcc305d0d08d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-278089/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1408d7e6-4ab7-505c-aa5f-e2eca124668f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-278089/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-278089/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-278089" :question/id #uuid "ef6815fb-0f6d-5589-bcfd-cadc0145d0fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-278089" :question/problem "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence with a positive common ratio is $2\\,304$, and the $6$th term is $36\\,864.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $496\\,800$ is the{{field-1}} th term." :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=2\\,304$ and ${a}_{6}=36\\,864$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{36\\,864}{2\\,304} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ r & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{4}=2\\,304$ and  $r=4$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{4-1} \\\\ 2\\,304 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\ 2\\,304 & =64{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =36\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $496\\,800.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >496\\,800 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >13\\,800 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈7.876.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $496\\,800.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-278089/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c8f1095-0b60-5dad-8471-4efa453e9d4b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-278089"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
